    {
      "question": "e-5560",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "9c9564e87685404ba4e38c113e21f3aaed9e340f7d6cb1611aaf99b9899e7eb6",
      "new_sha256": "de2600351487c62d1e8d536e059f59172eeabc90f14e390a5a7c7a36b313054a",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5560",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot 1=0,\\,\u2713 \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)=-6\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H.$\n\n- For the set $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})=0\\,\u2713 \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 0=0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5560.json",
          "file_sha256": "f3ff97016cf7aaf9c904ff0ffc065975a9b275757685ef5b2f4fb1618ae89ac2"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2191,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2192
    },
    {
      "question": "e-5561",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/problem",
      "old_sha256": "d89e3d7c9085ae6d6f1aa3330059ee318ca4ac193305a8837879de3dd7cc71b6",
      "new_sha256": "833a8e3926df76fb39cebb217483219581f74ffdaffc016292f08c134be540d6",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5561",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d648a1f36d090c680a6b72f30ff447ce78623f746157348f30a02fa4ea245c66.png) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d29f02d59f540cae1aae6057e05eeeb349d84de1ac9be11f6e3b131083f6905a.png) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5561.json",
          "file_sha256": "50cc7f35db98381827cc1bac49b9d002d0c531ede293542f0f096f7b97cb57f2"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2191,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2192
    },
    {
      "question": "e-5561",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "7064eddb577d9c87a44a2d721b0da250e1cf31eab3a4189f8b62226115433f23",
      "new_sha256": "d7e9eda299a5d2460aad772c45a13b8ec97cab4049b98de5a61a9c6cd5886bd8",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5561",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/608fd40128068aa5f05e6b256081bd2e0ebbf569fca4c9bae4977740eb4db2ce.png)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d6d7ea3c500d1112c64e19da288fbd7ad7e6ed912cf3121a2292427368b19f0e.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/c4875bc998184d934c9b43113cd31091b792f1c04ff4387d4b8ab8a7a08f90d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5561.json",
          "file_sha256": "50cc7f35db98381827cc1bac49b9d002d0c531ede293542f0f096f7b97cb57f2"
        }
      ],
