          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$   \n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-2)+8\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 3=0 & \u2713 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1=0 & \u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\".",
