          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/ed0e0a3a78ff25be588f62627ffd5bae4074f6c59b8e88ad160e4407253ae6dd.png) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/af507041d4aa3cc7dfb415afc672b5a7875f9f1a27acced81505797e5f92d7e0.png) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/34b29c1902a7c441b2ac15be91ffb60b8856ab7dc2706146d09eb02f081827ba.png)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/496154a4d67c0b7861da038306f1d5cc65188cc036cf8514b24603c7ce4cd948.png)",
